  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<ul><li><strong>Temperament:</strong> Peaceful. Can be kept in pairs or a group.</li><li><strong>Max size:</strong> 2.3 inches (about 5.8 cm)</li><li><strong>TDS:</strong> 60–200</li><li><strong>pH:</strong> 6.5–7.5</li><li><strong>Temperature:</strong> 75–85°F (24–29°C); much better coloration at warmer temperatures above 78°F (about 26°C)</li><li><strong>Diet:</strong> Carnivorous</li><li><strong>Breeding:</strong> Mouthbrooder</li><li><strong>IUCN Red List:</strong> Least Concern</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<p>Be sure to have the habitat ready before purchase.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<p>Another mouthbrooder type betta originating from South Kalimantan in Indonesia. The only one in its group, the Betta edithae complex.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<p>TDS: 60–200. They are kept in soft water with low TDS to match other wild bettas, but they are more tolerant of water conditions. pH: 6.5–7.5. Temperature: 75–85°F (24–29°C), with much better coloration at warmer temperatures above 78°F (about 26°C).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<p>Carnivorous. They are currently fed live blackworms, frozen bloodworms and pellets.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<p>This betta appears to be very peaceful and can be kept in groups. They can be maintained in a pair or a group.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<p>Males are generally larger with more iridescence on the body.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-edithae",
      "html": "<p>Mouthbrooder.</p>"
    }
